# A none stored in one record type's field is charged to a different type's
# field of the same name, in another module.
#
# Run: kanso check .      (or kanso run . / kanso run . --interp)
#
# Should: check ok, and print "<none> HI". cl's private `reading` holds a
# maybe-none `body`; lib's `request` is only ever built from a string, and
# `body_text` reads `request.body`.
#
# Does: refused, at a line in lib that never sees a none:
#   error[exhaustive]: this can be a none and `text/uppercase` has no arm for
#   it ... (in lib.kso) (module ./cl)
#     --> ./lib/lib.kso:10:19
# with no source line printed. `kanso check lib` says ok. Rename cl's field
# from `body` to `data` and the program checks and runs.
#
# In the httpd port, client/responses.kso stored a `text/slice` (maybe none
# since the 2026-10-13 gavel) in its private `reading.body`, and the refusal
# landed on http/request.kso's `body_text`, a function the client never
# calls.
import "./cl"

print (cl/read ["a"])
